==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*meh₂- (wet)}}
{{ety|en|:bor|la:ēmānāre<ety:af<ē<id:preposition>><mānāre<ety:af<mānō><-āre<id:verb suffix>>>>>|tree=++}}
From {{bor|en|la|ēmānāre||to flow out, spring out of, arise, proceed from}}, from {{m|la|ē||out}} + {{m|la|mānāre||to flow}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛm.əˌneɪt/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-emanate.ogg|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come from a source; [[issue]] from.
#: {{ux|en|Fragrance '''emanates''' from flowers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1837|author=w:Charles Dickens| title=w:The Pickwick Papers| url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/580/580-h/580-h.htm| passage={{...}} this Association has taken into its serious consideration a proposal, '''emanating''' from the aforesaid, Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., and three other Pickwickians hereinafter named, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1830|author=w:Thomas De Quincey|title=Kant in his Miscellaneous Essays|journal=Blackwood's Magazine
|passage=that subsisting form of government from which all special laws '''emanate'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=November 20, 1959|author={{w|Roald Dahl}}|title="The Landlady"|chapter=|url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/1959/11/28/the-landlady-fiction-roald-dahl |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260824064004/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/1959/11/28/the-landlady-fiction-roald-dahl |archivedate=24 August 2026 |page=|text=Now and again, he caught a whiff of a peculiar smell that seemed to '''emanate''' directly from her person. It was not in the least unpleasant, and it reminded him—well, he wasn’t quite sure what it reminded him of.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To send or give out; [[emit]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Charles Holdefer|title=Nice
|passage={{...}} his baggy, wrinkled suit '''emanating''' a diapery stench {{...}}}}

